WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.738
Welcome back to your Danish citizenship exam companion.

2
00:00:03.688 --> 00:00:10.152
This is Chapter Four, Episode Six: Danish foreign and security policy after the Cold War.

3
00:00:10.702 --> 00:00:12.352
Let's start with a shift.

4
00:00:12.302 --> 00:00:25.489
Even though Denmark, with its entry into NATO in 1949, gave up its traditional neutrality, Denmark kept a relatively low military profile until the end of the Cold War, around 1990.

5
00:00:25.489 --> 00:00:30.289
Denmark did, however, take part in many of the UN's peacekeeping missions.

6
00:00:30.289 --> 00:00:37.938
The task here was typically partly to help ensure that a ceasefire was observed, and partly to protect the civilian population.

7
00:00:38.488 --> 00:00:44.938
Now the period of foreign policy activism after the Cold War, 1990 to 2022.

8
00:00:44.888 --> 00:00:49.413
After the end of the Cold War, NATO's goals gradually changed.

9
00:00:49.413 --> 00:00:51.813
NATO remained a defence alliance.

10
00:00:51.813 --> 00:00:59.438
NATO is an international defence alliance, a forsvarsalliance, not a trade area and not a refugee cooperation.

11
00:00:59.438 --> 00:01:06.850
But the goal also increasingly became to promote peace, democracy, human rights and the rule of law around the world.

12
00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:17.688
In that connection it played a big role that NATO, after the end of the Cold War, has been clearly the world's strongest military and security-policy alliance.

13
00:01:17.688 --> 00:01:25.068
NATO, led by the USA, was thus less worried about being attacked or ending up in armed conflict with other countries.

14
00:01:25.618 --> 00:01:30.930
And Denmark developed, little by little, a so-called "activist foreign policy".

15
00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:37.693
That meant that Denmark began to take part more actively in international conflicts that went beyond peacekeeping missions.

16
00:01:37.693 --> 00:01:39.966
This was expressed in different ways.

17
00:01:40.516 --> 00:01:45.254
In 1990 to 91, Iraq occupied its neighbour Kuwait.

18
00:01:45.204 --> 00:01:51.066
On the basis of a UN decision, the USA led a military effort to liberate Kuwait.

19
00:01:51.066 --> 00:01:56.241
Denmark sent a warship as a contribution to the international military force.

20
00:01:56.241 --> 00:02:04.152
The ship did not take part in active combat, but had the task of enforcing the international community's trade blockade against Iraq.

21
00:02:04.702 --> 00:02:13.540
In connection with the civil war in Yugoslavia in the beginning of the 1990s, Danish soldiers were sent out as part of the UN forces.

22
00:02:13.490 --> 00:02:18.858
Here they were involved in direct combat with Serbian forces in Bosnia in 1994.

23
00:02:19.408 --> 00:02:26.695
Also in Kosovo, which at that time was a province in Serbia, there was civil war at the end of the 1990s.

24
00:02:26.645 --> 00:02:32.445
Here the Albanian population majority was subjected to abuses from the Serbian government.

25
00:02:32.445 --> 00:02:38.708
After long negotiations without result, NATO intervened militarily in 1999.

26
00:02:38.708 --> 00:02:42.012
Danish fighter planes took part in NATO's actions.

27
00:02:42.562 --> 00:02:47.224
Next, the efforts after the terror attack on the USA in 2001.

28
00:02:47.174 --> 00:02:57.337
A turning point for security policy in the NATO alliance, and Denmark among them, was the terror attack on the USA on the eleventh of September, 2001.

29
00:02:57.337 --> 00:03:07.312
Here terrorists killed about 3,000 people by hijacking passenger planes and flying them into, among other things, the World Trade Center in New York.

30
00:03:07.312 --> 00:03:13.350
After this terror attack, fighting terrorism became an important task for the alliance.

31
00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:25.825
After the terror attack, NATO took a new step and decided to strengthen its ability to fight any threat against the populations, territories and military forces of the member countries.

32
00:03:25.775 --> 00:03:28.950
This also applied to terror threats from terror groups.

33
00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:36.048
Denmark has therefore since 2001 taken part in military missions around the world that have had a focus on these goals.

34
00:03:36.598 --> 00:03:46.185
The terror organisation Al Qaeda, which was behind the attack on the USA on the eleventh of September, 2001, had its base in Afghanistan.

35
00:03:46.135 --> 00:03:58.598
This gave occasion for forces, led by NATO and with a mandate from the UN, to invade the country in October 2001 and depose the Islamist Taliban government, which cooperated with Al Qaeda.

36
00:03:58.598 --> 00:04:07.435
In 2002, a large majority in the Danish Folketing decided to send Danish soldiers to Afghanistan to take part in the war.

37
00:04:07.435 --> 00:04:15.882
For 20 years, the Taliban fought a guerrilla war against the international forces and the new, Western-supported Afghan government.

38
00:04:16.432 --> 00:04:26.544
The Danish soldiers were, among other things, stationed together with British troops in the southern Helmand province, which was one of the most unsettled in the country.

39
00:04:26.494 --> 00:04:31.119
In total 44 Danish soldiers lost their lives during the fighting.

40
00:04:31.119 --> 00:04:45.994
After 2014, the task of the Danish deployed soldiers was to train and support the Afghans so they themselves could take care of security in the country, and since then the Danish and other countries' soldiers have been withdrawn completely.

41
00:04:45.994 --> 00:04:52.332
As the international forces left Afghanistan, the country's government and army collapsed.

42
00:04:52.332 --> 00:04:59.019
In August 2021, the Taliban recaptured the capital Kabul and thereby power in the country.

43
00:04:59.019 --> 00:05:11.412
The West, including Denmark, started a historically extensive evacuation of its own diplomats, military advisers, and of Afghans who had worked for the West, such as interpreters, during the war.

44
00:05:11.962 --> 00:05:19.749
In 2003, the USA and a number of other countries fought a war against Iraq and the country's dictator Saddam Hussein.

45
00:05:19.699 --> 00:05:30.162
The war was justified by Iraq not living up to its obligations on military disarmament, which had been imposed on it by the UN after the Gulf War in 1991.

46
00:05:30.162 --> 00:05:36.274
In addition, the USA believed that Iraq had illegally developed weapons of mass destruction.

47
00:05:36.274 --> 00:05:40.374
However, no such weapons of mass destruction were ever found.

48
00:05:40.374 --> 00:05:45.862
This war was without a clear mandate from the UN, and it was not a NATO mission either.

49
00:05:45.862 --> 00:05:54.774
The war was carried out by an alliance of countries led by the USA and with the support of, among others, the United Kingdom and Denmark.

50
00:05:54.774 --> 00:06:01.518
Several NATO members, among them Germany, France and Norway, did not want to support the war.

51
00:06:02.068 --> 00:06:08.955
In Denmark it was only a small majority in the Folketing that voted for Denmark's participation in the Iraq War.

52
00:06:08.905 --> 00:06:12.805
Saddam Hussein was removed from power in 2003.

53
00:06:12.805 --> 00:06:23.693
After that the focus shifted to rebuilding Iraq after the war, to creating better living conditions for the Iraqis through humanitarian efforts, and to creating democracy.

54
00:06:23.693 --> 00:06:28.930
The Danish soldiers took part in this work together with soldiers from other countries.

55
00:06:28.930 --> 00:06:48.043
This work has, however, been made difficult by the removal of Saddam Hussein setting off a flare-up of several conflicts in Iraq between the country's different ethnic and religious groups, including the Sunni Muslim minority, which Saddam Hussein came from, and the Shia Muslim majority, which now gained power.

56
00:06:48.043 --> 00:06:51.793
At times the situation in Iraq has resembled civil war.

57
00:06:51.793 --> 00:06:58.200
In total, eight Danish soldiers died in connection with the American-led intervention in Iraq.

58
00:06:58.750 --> 00:07:00.062
And one more.

59
00:07:00.012 --> 00:07:06.000
In 2011, an uprising broke out against Libya's dictator Muammar al-Gaddafi.

60
00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:16.575
The uprising happened at the same time as uprisings in several neighbouring countries, for example Tunisia, Egypt and Syria, which became known as "the Arab Spring".

61
00:07:16.575 --> 00:07:22.387
A united Folketing decided that Denmark could take part in a military intervention in Libya.

62
00:07:22.387 --> 00:07:30.212
The intervention was, in accordance with a mandate from the UN, to protect the civilian population from abuses by Gaddafi.

63
00:07:30.212 --> 00:07:37.800
Denmark provided aircraft for the operation and carried out several bombings of Gaddafi's army and military bases.

64
00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:47.125
Subsequently Libya has been an extremely unstable country with weak governments, and where warring groups have been in something that is best described as a civil war.

65
00:07:47.125 --> 00:07:53.706
Libya has also become the starting point for many African migrants who seek to Europe across the Mediterranean.

66
00:07:54.256 --> 00:07:56.656
Here is your recap for Episode Six.

67
00:07:56.606 --> 00:08:05.481
Denmark joined NATO in 1949 but kept a low military profile until around 1990, taking part in UN peacekeeping.

68
00:08:05.481 --> 00:08:10.306
After the Cold War, Denmark developed an activist foreign policy.

69
00:08:10.306 --> 00:08:16.156
In 1990 to 91, a Danish warship enforced the trade blockade against Iraq.

70
00:08:16.156 --> 00:08:26.481
Throughout the Cold War, Denmark was not neutral and not allied with the Soviet Union in the Warsaw Pact: it was allied with the USA and the other Western countries through NATO.

71
00:08:26.481 --> 00:08:36.256
Danish soldiers fought Serbian forces in Bosnia in 1994, and Danish fighter planes took part in NATO's action in Kosovo in 1999.

72
00:08:36.256 --> 00:08:42.881
The terror attack on the USA on the eleventh of September, 2001, killed about 3,000 people.

73
00:08:42.881 --> 00:08:52.993
Al Qaeda was based in Afghanistan; NATO-led forces invaded in October 2001, and the Folketing decided in 2002 to send soldiers.

74
00:08:52.993 --> 00:09:00.168
44 Danish soldiers died in Afghanistan, and the Taliban retook Kabul in August 2021.

75
00:09:00.168 --> 00:09:12.193
In 2003, Denmark took part in the Iraq War, without a clear UN mandate and not as a NATO mission, with only a small majority in the Folketing; eight Danish soldiers died.

76
00:09:12.193 --> 00:09:19.860
In 2011, a united Folketing decided to take part in the intervention in Libya, with a UN mandate.

77
00:09:20.410 --> 00:09:23.010
Now it is time for your exam practice.

78
00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:27.872
These are real questions from past Danish citizenship exams on what you just heard.

79
00:09:27.872 --> 00:09:35.358
For each one, I will read the question and the choices, then pause so you can pick your answer, and then I will give you the correct answer.

80
00:09:35.908 --> 00:09:37.158
Question 1.

81
00:09:37.108 --> 00:09:38.346
What is NATO?

82
00:09:38.346 --> 00:09:41.946
Option A: An international free trade area.

83
00:09:41.946 --> 00:09:45.508
Option B: An international defence alliance.

84
00:09:45.508 --> 00:09:49.512
Option C: An international refugee cooperation.
[DA] Hvad er NATO?  A: Et internationalt frihandelsområde  |  B: En international forsvarsalliance  |  C: Et internationalt flygtningesamarbejde

85
00:09:56.062 --> 00:09:59.975
The answer is B: An international defence alliance.

86
00:09:59.924 --> 00:10:02.706
It appeared in the June 2022 exam.
[DA] ✔ B: En international forsvarsalliance

87
00:10:03.256 --> 00:10:04.506
Question 2.

88
00:10:04.456 --> 00:10:07.056
When did Denmark become a member of NATO?

89
00:10:07.056 --> 00:10:09.581
Option A: 1949.

90
00:10:09.581 --> 00:10:12.394
Option B: 1957.

91
00:10:12.394 --> 00:10:15.156
Option C: 1973.
[DA] Hvornår blev Danmark medlem af NATO?  A: 1949  |  B: 1957  |  C: 1973

92
00:10:21.706 --> 00:10:24.544
The answer is A: 1949.

93
00:10:24.494 --> 00:10:28.662
It appeared in the May 2023 and May 2025 exams.
[DA] ✔ A: 1949

94
00:10:29.212 --> 00:10:30.550
Question 3.

95
00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:34.825
What characterised Denmark's defence policy through most of the Cold War?

96
00:10:34.825 --> 00:10:38.787
Option A: Denmark was allied with the USA through NATO.

97
00:10:38.787 --> 00:10:41.425
Option B: Denmark was neutral.

98
00:10:41.425 --> 00:10:46.176
Option C: Denmark was allied with the Soviet Union through the Warsaw Pact.
[DA] Hvad kendetegnede Danmarks forsvarspolitik gennem det meste af Den Kolde Krig?  A: Danmark var allieret med USA gennem NATO  |  B: Danmark var neutralt  |  C: Danmark var allieret med Sovjetunionen gennem Warszawa-pagten

99
00:10:52.726 --> 00:10:56.876
The answer is A: Denmark was allied with the USA through NATO.

100
00:10:56.826 --> 00:11:01.362
It appeared in the November 2024 and June 2026 exams.
[DA] ✔ A: Danmark var allieret med USA gennem NATO

101
00:11:01.912 --> 00:11:03.212
Question 4.

102
00:11:03.162 --> 00:11:04.825
When did the Cold War end?

103
00:11:04.825 --> 00:11:07.687
Option A: Around 1950.

104
00:11:07.687 --> 00:11:10.462
Option B: Around 1970.

105
00:11:10.462 --> 00:11:13.380
Option C: Around 1990.
[DA] Hvornår ophørte Den Kolde Krig?  A: Omkring 1950  |  B: Omkring 1970  |  C: Omkring 1990

106
00:11:19.930 --> 00:11:22.905
The answer is C: Around 1990.

107
00:11:22.855 --> 00:11:25.686
It appeared in the June 2021 exam.
[DA] ✔ C: Omkring 1990

108
00:11:26.236 --> 00:11:27.699
Question 5.

109
00:11:27.649 --> 00:11:35.011
Which country did Denmark send soldiers to following the terror attack on the USA on the eleventh of September 2001?

110
00:11:35.011 --> 00:11:36.661
Option A: Mali.

111
00:11:36.661 --> 00:11:38.574
Option B: Kosovo.

112
00:11:38.574 --> 00:11:41.328
Option C: Afghanistan.
[DA] Hvilket land sendte Danmark soldater til på baggrund af terrorangrebet i USA den 11. september 2001?  A: Mali  |  B: Kosovo  |  C: Afghanistan

113
00:11:47.878 --> 00:11:50.803
The answer is C: Afghanistan.

114
00:11:50.753 --> 00:11:53.706
It appeared in the November 2023 exam.
[DA] ✔ C: Afghanistan

115
00:11:54.256 --> 00:11:55.694
Question 6.

116
00:11:55.644 --> 00:12:02.244
Which country did Denmark send soldiers to in 2003, following an American-led invasion?

117
00:12:02.244 --> 00:12:04.231
Option A: Syria.

118
00:12:04.231 --> 00:12:06.056
Option B: Iraq.

119
00:12:06.056 --> 00:12:07.908
Option C: Libya.
[DA] Hvilket land sendte Danmark soldater til i 2003 på baggrund af en amerikansk ledet invasion?  A: Syrien  |  B: Irak  |  C: Libyen

120
00:12:14.458 --> 00:12:16.583
The answer is B: Iraq.

121
00:12:16.533 --> 00:12:19.134
It appeared in the May 2024 exam.
[DA] ✔ B: Irak

122
00:12:19.684 --> 00:12:21.122
Question 7.

123
00:12:21.072 --> 00:12:26.472
Did the Danish government at the time support the American-led invasion of Iraq in 2003?

124
00:12:26.472 --> 00:12:28.097
Option A: Yes.

125
00:12:28.097 --> 00:12:29.760
Option B: No.
[DA] Støttede den daværende danske regering den amerikansk ledede invasion af Irak i 2003 (Irak-krigen)?  A: Ja  |  B: Nej

126
00:12:36.310 --> 00:12:38.098
The answer is A: Yes.

127
00:12:38.048 --> 00:12:42.210
It appeared in the May 2023 and May 2025 exams.
[DA] ✔ A: Ja

128
00:12:42.760 --> 00:12:44.523
That's the end of this part.

129
00:12:44.473 --> 00:12:45.540
Well done.

